   "source": [
    "Looking at the following plots, it is clear that outliers are present in the data. Removing outliers is a straightforward process that can be performed using iterative Grubbs' tests or Rosner tests. Initially, we should plan to include outliers in the modeling phase of the project. If the results appear clearly skewed or if the models show signs of overfitting, then we should remove the outliers and re-run the models. It is also important to note that tree-based models generally handle outliers better than linear regression models, which should be taken into consideration. Overall, the decision to remove outliers during model training should be left to the discretion of the modeler at each phase.\n",
    "\n",
    "When explaining financial impacts, all numerical data should be retained. Businesses need to be fully aware of outlier events in their operations and should consider them when analyzing financial outcomes. One exception to this guideline involves clear date anomalies, specifically, when an anchor date falls too far in the future. According to the data dictionary, the anchor_date represents the day of the week customers receive their orders. Therefore, any records with anchor_date values beyond 2026-01-01 will be removed."
   ]
  },
