   "source": [
    "## Reflection\n",
    "\n",
    "After going through the EDA above, one point stands clear. We have plenty of information at our disposal to draw insight into what predictors correlate with cart abandonment, what causes customers to return to an abandoned cart, and the effect it will have on Swire's financial bottom line. We focused on skews in the data, apparent patterns, the type of data we were given, and preparing for financial impacts.\n",
    " \n",
    "Skew: We spent a lot of time understanding skews in both the numerical data and observation count in the categorical data. Skews in both numerical and categorical data can lead to overfitted models that lack generalizability. Also, sparsity in categorical data can lead to results that are immaterial to the business. For example, the \"tablet\" observation in the device category column of the google analytics table barely shows up. If our models point to tablet users having a heavy correlation with cart abandonment, that technically solves the problem. However, tablet users are a tiny proportion of the customer base. This makes that insight immaterial. Understanding our skew and sparsity is paramount to contextualizing the predictors we find for cart abandonment.\n",
    " \n",
    "Patterns: We made sure to analyze individual tables and linked tables to try and unearth the more overt patterns in the dataset. Preliminary patterns revealed during the EDA, such as the frequency of repeated visits, will help highlight potential recovery behaviors. Linking customer IDs or session identifiers across order cycles, can show us whether users who abandoned carts later return to complete an order, shift to a different channel, or churn completely. Below are a handful of patterns we noticed in the dataset.\n",
    "\n",
    "1. User Behavior Patterns: Desktop dominates total traffic and conversions, while mobile has the highest abandonment rate. This indicates the checkout experience is optimized for desktops but less seamless on smaller devices. Perhaps Swire could revamp the mobile UI, but it is too early to tell at this stage.\n",
    "2. Funnel Drop off Insights: The largest engagement gap occurs between add_to_cart and begin_checkout. Many customers add items but never proceed likely due to friction on mobile or missing contextual reminders before their anchor day.\n",
    "3. Temporal Trends: Activity is strongest Monday to Thursday and around late afternoon (local time). Orders also spike in Q3 2024, then taper off. This suggests seasonality.\n",
    "4. Operational Factors: The visit plan and cutoff-time data confirm that missed anchor days often align with abandoned sessions. Customers with shorter shipping windows (24–48 hrs) or earlier cutoff times may need earlier reminders or extended flexibility.\n",
    "5. Timeframes: We noticed that (as you can imagine) most sales activity happens during the week, along with seasonality in profits/revenue.\n",
    " \n",
    "Type of Data: We have been provided with panel data. We have historical customer information across various types, regions, stores, etc.. displayed over time. This gives us enough historical information to model out what might cause customers to return to a previously abandoned cart. We will need to rely heavily on the data from the google analytics and visit plan tables to model out the results of why customers return to their abandoned carts.\n",
    " \n",
    "Preparing for Financial Impacts: It was observed that numerous records within the visit plan lacked distribution mode values, which complicates accurate order window framing. To address this, the analysis defaults to the 5 PM cutoff time from the cutoff_times table; however, this introduces potential minor misrepresentations in temporal revenue framing. While these deviations are unlikely to significantly distort aggregate outcomes, they could affect short-term window-based revenue estimations and should be accounted for in model interpretation. The potential misalignment in order window timing may also introduce marginal distortions in revenue recognition timing, which could influence how cart abandonment correlates with financial performance metrics. Nonetheless, the overall model robustness is expected to remain strong given proper handling of temporal variables. Outlier financial values, while present, are not anticipated to materially impact modeling performance, provided appropriate preprocessing and the use of models tolerant to heteroscedasticity and scale variance."
   ]
  },
